A||||12||Jncomutt||What Clutch Parts To Replace With New Clutch?||||1||
Z||000000||Jncomutt||08-19-2001||02:31 PM||||I am purchasing a new clutch from The Fiero Store.  Its the Heavy Duty Ram Clutch.  I'm having it installed at a shop and was wondering if theres anything else I should buy to have them change while its apart.  I just don't want to have to have it back in the shop a month later for something that could be changed out now.  The Kit come with the pressure plate, the clutch disc, and a new throw out bearing.  <A HREF="http://www.secureleadercom.com/store/prodinfo_pegasus.asp?number=54002&variation=&aitem=10&mitem=18" TARGET=_blank>http://www.secureleadercom.com/store/prodinfo_pegasus.asp?number=54002&variation=&aitem=10&mitem=18</A> <P>When I press my clutch sometimes it really hard to push down.  It will engage fine and all but requires pressure.  I have broke a few clutch arms I think because of this.  If I race it however, or drop the clutch from about 4000 it will be easy to push back down again.  Any suggestions for why this is?  Or will it fix with the new kit?||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||08-19-2001||03:08 PM||||there are two kinds of release forks. one is welded up bunch of pieces (junk) other is one piece forged. make sure you have or get the forged one, also takes a different pivot shaft. Make sure its greased with waterproof lithium grease along shaft because once its in you cant again.||||reg||1
Z||000002||LS1swap||08-19-2001||08:47 PM||||dosn't have to do with the clutch, but new trans mounts would be a good idea||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||08-19-2001||09:35 PM||||Not to discourage you, but: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006281.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006281.html</A> <P>My RAM clutch is also very jittery until thoroughly warmed up, at least 10 minutes of driving in city. It is so bad, it rattles the dash and almost my teeth out until warmed up. It is possibly to engauge the clutch a little more smoother when its cold, but its a PITA to drive until warmed up. Then, once its warmed up, I have the problems mentioned in the above link.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jncomutt||08-19-2001||10:49 PM||||The only reason I have the RAM in mind is becuase its the only option I know of.  If anyone has any recommendations I am very willing to pick up on them.  I just didn't know of anything else.<P>Also... I think I just Blew it up, if thats possible.  I was driving it hard thru a corner, and the clutch started to slip but I kept on the gas.  Now, when ever I give it just a little gas, the rear end bucks like hell.  It Shakes sooo bad until the clutch is fully out.  Then it will be fine as it idles around... anytime you give it gas, it slips, and the banging shake comes back.  What the hell did I just do??||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jncomutt||08-20-2001||09:54 PM||||no suggestions??<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||08-20-2001||10:43 PM||||If a V-6, the rear main seal is a good candidae, the rear cam cover gasket may be leaking, the oil pan gasket may be leaking.   I put in a high volume oil pump since I had the pan off. with 170,000 miles, I figured it was a good idea for $70.  Transaxle and motor mounts are easier with the assembly out of the car, and under $10 a piece at Autozone (they actually fit, too unlike some other brands...).  Good time to get the plugs and fix any exhaust leaks, too.  Poly cradle bushings, anyone?  Don't forget new axle shaft seals.<BR>These will all cost an arm and a leg for labor, but some things they should replace as part of the job, like the axle shaft seals.  If you can do them yourself, the aren't difficult to do, just a lot easier with the engine/transaxle/cradle asembly out of the car.||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||08-20-2001||10:48 PM||||As far as clutch parts go, I have a stock V-6 and Getrag in mine, and I got 60,000 out of the first two OEM clutches, 50,000 out of the RAM/OEM replacement ($130 from Summit Racing), and now I have a standard no-name stock replacement ($120, bought locally).  The RAM and the no-name brand had the exact same pressure plate (LUK) that the Pontiac dealer put in the first time I had it replaced, only at about 1/4 the price...<P>I beat the crap out of my clutches, too.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||08-21-2001||01:27 PM||||I have stock OEM replacement clutch. I have modified 3.1 with turbo. I drive hard and fast but dont abuse it. Still works perfectly after 87,000 miles. Burnouts just cost money.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||08-21-2001||09:31 PM||||I've heard the Getrag's input shaft bearing is a good thing to replace if it's been a while, any expierence with this guys?<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000010||GSXPete||08-21-2001||10:04 PM||||rockey hill, eh? i live in hartford. IM me or email me at TmGreen01@aol.com||||reg||1
Z||000011||Jimmy||08-21-2001||10:49 PM||||I think the Centerforce Dual Friction clutch that I am using would be ideal for a 2.8.  I paid $240.00 for my clutch disc and centerforce pressure palte, the T.O. bearing was an extra $35.00.  This IMHO is a better clutch than the RAM clutch, not to mention it has a lighter pedal feel to it.<P>Jim<BR>87 GT<BR>87 GT-TOP V8||||reg||1
Z||000012||Old Lar||08-22-2001||09:33 AM||||Twin Lakes replaced the two clutch cables when he replaced the clutch.   Pricey little items. <A HREF="http://www.gmpartsdirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.gmpartsdirect.com</A>  had the in stock for 1/2 the price of my local Mr Goodwrench.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 08-22-2001).]||||reg||1
